1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:03,161
Day thirty. You've eaten fast food, and

2
00:00:03,161 --> 00:00:06,300
pretty much only fast food, for a month.

3
00:00:06,850 --> 00:00:09,228
Your liver is sending distress signals.

4
00:00:09,228 --> 00:00:12,337
In one study, liver blood tests jumped dramatically

5
00:00:12,337 --> 00:00:14,837
in under four weeks, in perfectly healthy

6
00:00:14,837 --> 00:00:15,630
young people.

7
00:00:16,180 --> 00:00:18,227
You've gained around six kilos.

8
00:00:18,227 --> 00:00:20,340
That part you probably expected.

9
00:00:20,890 --> 00:00:23,406
What you didn't expect is the part that

10
00:00:23,406 --> 00:00:25,406
shows up a year after you stop.

11
00:00:25,406 --> 00:00:28,309
Because a month of fast food leaves something

12
00:00:28,309 --> 00:00:30,825
behind. So let's do it properly, day by

13
00:00:30,825 --> 00:00:32,889
day, with your vitals on screen.

14
00:00:32,889 --> 00:00:33,470
Order up.

15
00:00:34,020 --> 00:00:36,709
You're not the first person to try this.

16
00:00:36,709 --> 00:00:39,330
In two thousand three, filmmaker Morgan

17
00:00:39,330 --> 00:00:41,951
Spurlock ate only McDonald's for thirty

18
00:00:41,951 --> 00:00:44,640
days for his documentary, Super Size Me.

19
00:00:44,640 --> 00:00:45,850
Three meals a day.

20
00:00:45,850 --> 00:00:48,337
Every item on the menu at least once.

21
00:00:48,337 --> 00:00:51,025
And if they offered to super size it, he

22
00:00:51,025 --> 00:00:51,630
said yes.

23
00:00:52,180 --> 00:00:54,750
That added up to around five thousand calories

24
00:00:54,750 --> 00:00:57,153
a day, and he cut his walking to about five

25
00:00:57,153 --> 00:00:59,779
thousand steps, roughly what a typical American

26
00:00:59,779 --> 00:01:00,170
walked.

27
00:01:00,720 --> 00:01:03,559
But one man with a camera isn't science.

28
00:01:03,559 --> 00:01:06,327
So a few years later, doctors in Sweden

29
00:01:06,327 --> 00:01:07,675
ran a proper study.

30
00:01:07,675 --> 00:01:10,656
Eighteen healthy young adults, average age

31
00:01:10,656 --> 00:01:13,495
twenty six. At least two fast food meals

32
00:01:13,495 --> 00:01:16,760
a day. The goal, double their normal calories.

33
00:01:17,310 --> 00:01:19,988
And no exercise. They had to stay under

34
00:01:19,988 --> 00:01:21,773
five thousand steps a day.

35
00:01:21,773 --> 00:01:24,794
They even had a matched control group living

36
00:01:24,794 --> 00:01:26,510
normally, for comparison.

37
00:01:27,060 --> 00:01:29,047
So that's your challenge.

38
00:01:29,047 --> 00:01:32,386
Thirty days. Burgers, fries, fizzy drinks,

39
00:01:32,386 --> 00:01:35,010
and a couch. What could go wrong?

40
00:01:35,560 --> 00:01:37,504
Day one is fantastic.

41
00:01:37,504 --> 00:01:38,430
Obviously.

42
00:01:38,980 --> 00:01:41,790
But here's what's happening on the inside.

43
00:01:41,790 --> 00:01:44,466
In one experiment, a single nine hundred

44
00:01:44,466 --> 00:01:47,477
calorie, high fat meal cut how well an artery

45
00:01:47,477 --> 00:01:49,953
in the arm could widen by about half.

46
00:01:49,953 --> 00:01:51,090
Within two hours.

47
00:01:51,640 --> 00:01:54,075
It was still down four hours later.

48
00:01:54,075 --> 00:01:57,067
Your blood vessels need to be able to relax

49
00:01:57,067 --> 00:01:59,780
and widen. After a meal like this, they

50
00:01:59,780 --> 00:02:01,241
get stiff, for hours.

51
00:02:01,241 --> 00:02:03,050
Then you eat the next one.

52
00:02:03,600 --> 00:02:06,468
In the same study, fat levels in the blood,

53
00:02:06,468 --> 00:02:09,069
called triglycerides, rose by more than

54
00:02:09,069 --> 00:02:11,670
half, two hours after that single meal.

55
00:02:12,220 --> 00:02:13,569
And then there's salt.

56
00:02:13,569 --> 00:02:16,082
The World Health Organization says adults

57
00:02:16,082 --> 00:02:18,535
should have less than five grams of salt

58
00:02:18,535 --> 00:02:20,742
a day. That's just under a teaspoon.

59
00:02:20,742 --> 00:02:23,440
Burger meals are not designed with teaspoons

60
00:02:23,440 --> 00:02:23,930
in mind.

61
00:02:24,480 --> 00:02:27,064
Day three. Meet the trillions of microbes

62
00:02:27,064 --> 00:02:28,262
living in your gut.

63
00:02:28,262 --> 00:02:30,720
They're about to have a very bad month.

64
00:02:31,270 --> 00:02:34,216
In a study in the journal Nature, changing

65
00:02:34,216 --> 00:02:37,162
what people ate changed their gut bacteria

66
00:02:37,162 --> 00:02:40,109
within days. On a diet of mostly meat, fat

67
00:02:40,109 --> 00:02:43,055
and cheese, fibre-loving bacteria dropped,

68
00:02:43,055 --> 00:02:45,510
and bile-loving bacteria took over.

69
00:02:46,060 --> 00:02:48,767
One of the winners was a microbe that scientists

70
00:02:48,767 --> 00:02:50,966
have linked to inflammation in the gut.

71
00:02:50,966 --> 00:02:53,165
Your new gut residents love what you're

72
00:02:53,165 --> 00:02:54,970
eating. That's not a compliment.

73
00:02:55,520 --> 00:02:59,856
Around day five, Spurlock reported feeling

74
00:02:59,856 --> 00:03:03,470
awful. Depressed. Tired. Headaches.

75
00:03:04,020 --> 00:03:06,737
And the only thing that seemed to fix it?

76
00:03:06,737 --> 00:03:08,791
Eating another McDonald's meal.

77
00:03:08,791 --> 00:03:11,375
He'd feel better, for a while, and then

78
00:03:11,375 --> 00:03:12,170
crash again.

79
00:03:12,720 --> 00:03:15,155
Now, one honest note before we go on.

80
00:03:15,155 --> 00:03:17,918
Years later, in twenty seventeen, Spurlock

81
00:03:17,918 --> 00:03:20,550
admitted he'd also been drinking heavily

82
00:03:20,550 --> 00:03:23,709
during the experiment, which he hadn't disclosed

83
00:03:23,709 --> 00:03:26,275
in the film. So some of his results are

84
00:03:26,275 --> 00:03:26,670
messy.

85
00:03:27,220 --> 00:03:30,593
That's exactly why the Swedish study matters.

86
00:03:30,593 --> 00:03:33,142
Normal people. No secret drinking.

87
00:03:33,142 --> 00:03:36,590
Weekly blood tests. Let's follow them instead.

88
00:03:37,140 --> 00:03:40,220
By the end of week one, the scale is moving.

89
00:03:40,220 --> 00:03:41,970
And it's not moving down.

90
00:03:42,520 --> 00:03:45,167
Spurlock put on around four and a half kilos

91
00:03:45,167 --> 00:03:46,550
in his first five days.

92
00:03:47,100 --> 00:03:50,011
And here's the thing about overeating.

93
00:03:50,011 --> 00:03:52,462
Your body can only burn so much.

94
00:03:52,462 --> 00:03:55,526
The rest gets stored, as fat, under your

95
00:03:55,526 --> 00:03:58,514
skin, and, more worryingly, inside your

96
00:03:58,514 --> 00:03:59,050
organs.

97
00:03:59,600 --> 00:04:01,875
Fast food also makes it easy to overeat

98
00:04:01,875 --> 00:04:04,207
without noticing. In a hospital study at

99
00:04:04,207 --> 00:04:06,773
the US National Institutes of Health, people

100
00:04:06,773 --> 00:04:09,106
offered ultra-processed meals ate around

101
00:04:09,106 --> 00:04:11,439
five hundred more calories a day than on

102
00:04:11,439 --> 00:04:13,772
unprocessed food, when they could eat as

103
00:04:13,772 --> 00:04:14,880
much as they liked.

104
00:04:15,430 --> 00:04:18,216
They gained almost a kilo in two weeks on

105
00:04:18,216 --> 00:04:20,933
the ultra-processed diet, and lost about

106
00:04:20,933 --> 00:04:23,108
the same on the unprocessed one.

107
00:04:23,108 --> 00:04:25,010
Same people. Different food.

108
00:04:25,560 --> 00:04:28,343
Week three. Back in Sweden, the doctors

109
00:04:28,343 --> 00:04:30,556
check everyone's liver enzymes.

110
00:04:30,556 --> 00:04:32,411
Especially one called ALT.

111
00:04:32,411 --> 00:04:35,337
When liver cells are damaged or stressed,

112
00:04:35,337 --> 00:04:37,050
it leaks into the blood.

113
00:04:37,600 --> 00:04:39,973
And ALT levels rose dramatically.

114
00:04:39,973 --> 00:04:43,280
The researchers called it a rapid and profound

115
00:04:43,280 --> 00:04:45,365
elevation. In healthy people.

116
00:04:45,365 --> 00:04:47,090
In less than four weeks.

117
00:04:47,640 --> 00:04:50,216
And the strongest link they found wasn't

118
00:04:50,216 --> 00:04:52,471
the burgers, exactly. It was sugar.

119
00:04:52,471 --> 00:04:54,983
How much sugar people ate in week three

120
00:04:54,983 --> 00:04:58,010
predicted how high their liver enzymes climbed.

121
00:04:58,560 --> 00:05:01,070
Fat inside their livers more than doubled

122
00:05:01,070 --> 00:05:03,763
on average, from about one percent to almost

123
00:05:03,763 --> 00:05:04,130
three.

124
00:05:04,680 --> 00:05:07,305
Their advice to other doctors was blunt.

125
00:05:07,305 --> 00:05:09,996
If a patient has bad liver results, don't

126
00:05:09,996 --> 00:05:11,505
just ask about alcohol.

127
00:05:11,505 --> 00:05:14,130
Ask if they've recently been overeating.

128
00:05:14,680 --> 00:05:16,880
Day thirty. The finish line.

129
00:05:17,430 --> 00:05:19,974
The Swedish volunteers gained six point

130
00:05:19,974 --> 00:05:22,517
four kilos on average, going from about

131
00:05:22,517 --> 00:05:25,387
sixty seven point six to seventy four kilos.

132
00:05:25,387 --> 00:05:26,300
In four weeks.

133
00:05:26,850 --> 00:05:29,532
Spurlock gained eleven point one kilos,

134
00:05:29,532 --> 00:05:32,420
about thirteen percent of his body weight.

135
00:05:32,420 --> 00:05:35,309
His cholesterol went up to two hundred and

136
00:05:35,309 --> 00:05:38,334
thirty. He also reported mood swings, sexual

137
00:05:38,334 --> 00:05:40,260
problems, and a fatty liver.

138
00:05:40,810 --> 00:05:42,123
And then it's over.

139
00:05:42,123 --> 00:05:43,990
You go back to normal food.

140
00:05:43,990 --> 00:05:45,649
You start walking again.

141
00:05:45,649 --> 00:05:47,930
And the weight starts coming off.

142
00:05:48,480 --> 00:05:51,728
Six months later, the Swedish team checked

143
00:05:51,728 --> 00:05:54,203
in. Most of the weight was gone.

144
00:05:54,203 --> 00:05:54,590
Phew.

145
00:05:55,140 --> 00:05:57,997
A year later, they scanned everyone's bodies.

146
00:05:57,997 --> 00:06:00,663
Their muscle was back to where it started.

147
00:06:00,663 --> 00:06:03,202
But their body fat was still higher than

148
00:06:03,202 --> 00:06:05,932
before the experiment. About one point four

149
00:06:05,932 --> 00:06:06,630
kilos more.

150
00:06:07,180 --> 00:06:10,045
Two and a half years later, they were still

151
00:06:10,045 --> 00:06:12,976
about three kilos heavier than at the start.

152
00:06:12,976 --> 00:06:15,708
The control group, who never did the fast

153
00:06:15,708 --> 00:06:17,840
food month? Basically unchanged.

154
00:06:18,390 --> 00:06:20,645
The researchers said the extra fat was more

155
00:06:20,645 --> 00:06:22,794
than you'd expect from normal ageing, and

156
00:06:22,794 --> 00:06:24,892
raised the question of whether one short

157
00:06:24,892 --> 00:06:27,094
month of overeating could have a long term

158
00:06:27,094 --> 00:06:28,090
effect on body fat.

159
00:06:28,640 --> 00:06:31,527
It's a small study, eighteen people, so

160
00:06:31,527 --> 00:06:34,192
it's a question, not a final answer.

161
00:06:34,192 --> 00:06:36,413
But it's an uncomfortable one.

162
00:06:36,413 --> 00:06:39,300
One month. A souvenir that lasts years.

163
00:06:39,850 --> 00:06:42,402
So, eat fast food for thirty days, and your

164
00:06:42,402 --> 00:06:44,716
blood vessels stiffen after every meal,

165
00:06:44,716 --> 00:06:47,387
your gut bugs get replaced, your liver sounds

166
00:06:47,387 --> 00:06:49,998
the alarm in under four weeks, and you might

167
00:06:49,998 --> 00:06:52,550
carry a little bit of that month for years.

168
00:06:53,100 --> 00:06:55,911
Does that mean never have a burger again?

169
00:06:55,911 --> 00:06:58,997
No. Everything in this video was about eating

170
00:06:58,997 --> 00:07:01,945
mostly fast food, way too much of it, while

171
00:07:01,945 --> 00:07:04,413
barely moving. One burger is a meal.

172
00:07:04,413 --> 00:07:07,430
Thirty days of them is a science experiment.

173
00:07:07,980 --> 00:07:09,330
That's month one of Your Vitals.

174
00:07:09,330 --> 00:07:10,300
Next month, you choose.

175
00:07:10,300 --> 00:07:12,029
Comment the worst idea you want your body

176
00:07:12,029 --> 00:07:13,801
to survive, and the most liked one becomes

177
00:07:13,801 --> 00:07:15,530
the next episode. See you on the monitor.
